[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Distributed Tracing and Context Propagation in Microservices"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a microservices architecture, a single user request often spans multiple distinct services, databases, and message queues. Debugging performance bottlenecks or errors across these distributed boundaries requires a unified view of the request lifecycle. This tutorial explores distributed tracing and context propagation using OpenTelemetry and the W3C Trace Context standard."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding W3C Trace Context"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The W3C Trace Context specification standardizes how tracing data is propagated across network boundaries. It defines two primary HTTP headers:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"traceparent: Identifies the incoming request in a globally distributed system. It contains the version, trace-id, parent-id (or span-id), and trace-flags."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"tracestate: Carries vendor-specific diagnostic information as a list of key-value pairs, ensuring interoperability between different APM vendors."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"OpenTelemetry Architecture"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"OpenTelemetry (OTel) is a vendor-neutral observability framework providing APIs and SDKs to generate, collect, and export telemetry data (traces, metrics, and logs). Context propagation is the mechanism by which trace data is injected into outgoing requests and extracted from incoming requests."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Context Propagation in Node.js"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following example demonstrates how to initialize OpenTelemetry and propagate the tracing context manually using HTTP requests in a Node.js microservice."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"const { NodeTracerProvider } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-node');
const { trace, context, propagation } = require('@opentelemetry/api');
const http = require('http');

const provider = new NodeTracerProvider();
provider.register();

const tracer = trace.getTracer('my-microservice');

function makeOutgoingRequest(url) {
  const span = tracer.startSpan('outgoing-call');

  context.with(trace.setSpan(context.active(), span), () => {
    const headers = {};
    propagation.inject(context.active(), headers);

    // Headers now contain the W3C traceparent and tracestate
    console.log('Injected headers:', headers);

    span.end();
  });
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Extracting Context on the Receiving Service"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a downstream service receives an HTTP request, it must extract the W3C headers to continue the trace rather than starting a new one."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"function handleIncomingRequest(req, res) {
  // Extract context from incoming HTTP headers
  const extractedContext = propagation.extract(context.active(), req.headers);

  const span = tracer.startSpan('incoming-call', undefined, extractedContext);

  context.with(trace.setSpan(context.active(), span), () => {
    try {
      // Process business logic here
      res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
      res.end(JSON.stringify({ status: 'success' }));
    } finally {
      span.end();
    }
  });
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Distributed Tracing"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use automatic instrumentation libraries provided by OpenTelemetry for frameworks like Express, FastAPI, or Spring Boot to avoid manual propagation boilerplate."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implement tail-based sampling in your collector architecture to capture error traces while dropping high-volume successful traces."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sanitize attributes added to spans to avoid leaking Personally Identifiable Information (PII) or secrets."}]
